# Defaults for an *interactive* Anaconda run.
#
# Anaconda reads this file only when no kickstart is passed on the command
# line (no inst.ks=). Unlike a real kickstart it does not set the automated
# install flag, so the installer still stops and asks about anything left
# unconfigured -- the disk, in our case.
#
# @PAYLOAD_REF@ is substituted by Containerfile.installer from the PAYLOAD_REF
# build argument, so the reference always matches what --bootc-installer-payload-ref
# embedded in the ISO.
#
# Why these two lines are needed:
#
# 1. Payload. There is no inst.ks= telling Anaconda what to install, so the
#    bootc container has to be named here. The ISO carries a copy of it in its
#    container storage (--bootc-installer-payload-ref), hence the
#    containers-storage: transport. --target-imgref must NOT carry a transport
#    prefix (pykickstart rejects one); it is the reference the installed system
#    tracks for future updates.
#
# 2. Desktop. With no explicit target Anaconda guesses: it looks for a package
#    providing service(graphical-login) in the target and falls back to
#    multi-user.target when it cannot prove one exists -- which boots the
#    installed system to a console. `xconfig --startxonboot` pins
#    graphical.target regardless. (Same workaround as bootc-image-builder
#    issue #918, on a Silverblue-derived payload.)
#
# Deliberately absent: every storage directive. No clearpart, no autopart, no
# zerombr. Leaving storage untouched is what makes Anaconda show the
# Installation Destination page (pick the disk, keep Automatic, Done) instead
# of silently wiping the first disk the way the kickstart-driven
# bootc-installer build did.
bootc --source-imgref containers-storage:@PAYLOAD_REF@ --target-imgref @PAYLOAD_REF@

xconfig --startxonboot
